# Line-ending policy.
#
# TEXT IS STORED AND CHECKED OUT AS LF, on every machine, by committed rule rather than by the machine's
# `core.autocrlf`. That matters for a release: a materialised release is consumed inside a Git work tree
# whose `git add` applies THIS file, and the platform's own self-proof compares what the work tree records
# with the manifest. Without a committed rule, a consumer on a machine that defaults to
# `core.autocrlf=true` records DIFFERENT bytes from the same files, so the same release would prove itself
# on one machine and fail on another. The specific rules BELOW override this one (the last matching
# pattern wins), which is how the byte-fidelity trees stay verbatim.
* text=auto eol=lf

# `instruction-manuals/` holds the DISTRIBUTED copies of the master instruction
# manuals published by the upstream project. They must stay byte-identical to
# their master source and are verified with SHA-256 (procedure in that
# directory's README). Automatic line-ending conversion would silently break
# that contract on any machine with `core.autocrlf=true`, so the manuals are
# stored and checked out verbatim — no text conversion, ever.
#
# Edit the MASTER upstream and re-propagate; never edit a distributed copy in place.
instruction-manuals/** -text

# Asset artwork is verified BYTE-FOR-BYTE.
#
# The authored asset SOURCE trees, and the GENERATED runtime namespaces they are mirrored into, are
# stored verbatim in EVERY layout Foundation supports. The layout vocabulary itself lives in ONE place
# (`src/config/deployment-root.ts`, `DEPLOYMENT_RESOURCE_PATHS.assetSources`), so these patterns spell
# the layouts it defines and nothing else:
#
#   content/assets/**                       a repository-root Installation (a standalone spoke)
#   deployment/content/assets/**            a capsule whose Installation root owns the trees itself
#   deployment/spokes/*/content/assets/**   a capsule whose Spokes each own their authored root
#   public/assets/**                        the shared PLATFORM runtime namespace
#   public/spokes/*/assets/**               a Spoke's own runtime namespace
#
# The mirror proof is a SHA-256 comparison of source and derivative as they exist on disk
# (`scripts/sync-runtime-assets.mjs`, `pnpm assets:check`,
# `deployment/tests/unit/asset-taxonomy-mirror.test.ts`), and the favicon contract compares the branding
# mark with the favicon derived from it — so no automatic line-ending conversion may rewrite either side.
#
# Every supported SOURCE layout must stay listed: a rule for a tree that stops existing would silently
# stop protecting artwork (FOUNDATION-PAGES-A1D moved the source tree from `assets/` to
# `content/assets/`, ISO-B2B moved it into the deployment capsule, and ISO-B3C2 made the capsule author a
# PER-SPOKE root). The runtime namespaces are GENERATED and NOT version-controlled
# (FOUNDATION-DEPLOYMENT-ISO-B3C1): their rules are kept as guards so that an explicit re-add — or a
# downstream repository that still commits a mirror — can never have its artwork bytes normalised.
# `deployment/tests/unit/asset-taxonomy-mirror.test.ts` asserts that every one of these patterns carries
# `-text`, and that no rule survives for a retired tree.
content/assets/** -text
deployment/content/assets/** -text
deployment/spokes/*/content/assets/** -text
public/assets/** -text
public/spokes/*/assets/** -text

# THE PINNED CONNECT SOURCE TREE IS VERIFIED BYTE-FOR-BYTE
# (FOUNDATION-CONNECT-MODULE-INTEGRATION-A9).
#
# `modules/connect/src/**` is the released Connect source, carried verbatim: Connect is a Git-SOURCE
# Module, so there is no package, no registry artifact and no archive to depend on.
# `scripts/connect/verify-pinned-source.mjs` recomputes the Git tree object id of that tree and
# compares it with the id recorded in `modules/connect/connect-release.json`.
#
# Automatic line-ending conversion would rewrite the bytes and break that proof — and it has already
# happened once: Connect publishes no `.gitattributes` of its own, so the upstream CLONE on a
# `core.autocrlf=true` machine arrives as CRLF, and `git archive` converts as well. A vendor step that
# copies a working tree therefore carries bytes the release never published. Store and check out
# verbatim, exactly like `instruction-manuals/**` and `LICENSE`, and materialise from
# `git cat-file blob` when re-vendoring.
modules/connect/src/** -text


